## For the lead

1. **After-core check days (calendar pass).** This course's checks are course days 29 (school day 73, week 15) and 63
   (school day 158, week 32), off every grade 8 core mid-year check day; week 32 is the finals week. Other after-core
   overlaps are for the one calendar pass, not moved here (the kindergarten pilot's For the lead 1 pick).
2. **The partial base's Year 9 units and store folder.** Same item as the kindergarten frame's For the lead 4: this
   frame maps three archived Year 9 units from their public pages (lesson titles and unit overviews, opened
   2026-11-25). When the store folder is made, the lead reads the Python sequences, data science and cybersecurity
   lesson files, confirms the placements, and checks the cybersecurity lessons 3 and 5 left out by title (they may
   hold a protection-only part that fits day 5). The Python unit's overview names leaked passwords as an example data
   set: that example is left out under STANDARD.md "Privacy and safety".
3. **Physical computing left out (scope, Cory's).** The archived Year 9 physical computing unit needs a board;
   STANDARD.md makes a board optional, and no grade 8 row needs one. Pick: leave it out of grade 8; a school with boards
   may run it as an extension outside the course's days.
4. **Lists inside C1.** The list days (about 4 of 64, about 6 percent) count as days outside the standards under C1,
   with any other such days; the grade 8 scope and sequence keeps the total under 10 percent.
5. **Source list.** docs/APPROACH-SOURCES.md, beside the scout's Teach Computing entry: the archived key stage 3 page
   (https://www.raspberrypi.org/curriculum/archived/key-stage-3); the W3C WAI "How People with Disabilities Use the
   Web" page; the CISA "Recognize and Report Phishing" page; Mitchell and others 2019, Model Cards for Model
   Reporting; the Office of the Assistant Secretary for Planning and Evaluation's 2022 telehealth report; the National Digital Inclusion Alliance; the FTC's Fair Credit Reporting Act page; Lawrence Berkeley National
   Laboratory's 2024 United States Data Center Energy Usage Report. Each fact is checked at source when its day is
   written.
6. **Row changes** (US spelling, a settled math lean, a named case, and the sensitivity fix to the design-for-all
   task) are listed in my final message for the lead to apply.

## Source map

The partial base for grade 8 is the archived edition of England's Teach Computing Curriculum, Year 9 (six units;
raspberrypi.org/curriculum/archived/key-stage-3, resources dated 2023). Placed from the public unit pages (lesson
titles and unit overviews), opened 2026-11-25; each archived Year 9 unit has six lessons. One
Year 8 lesson the grade 7 frame offered is used too; every other Year 8 unit is the grade 7 frame's. The publisher teaches each
unit's lessons in number order; where this frame splits or reorders a unit, the row says why. Each adapted lesson
keeps its idea, is rewritten in our words for an American grade 8, replaces named apps and data sets with our editor,
our pages and invented data, and is credited once a day. Check days are ours, never a kept lesson.

| Base unit (Year 9 unless named) | Lessons | Placed in | How it is used | Left out, and why |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Introduction of cybersecurity | 2 Social engineering; 4 Rise of the bots; 6 Under attack | Unit 1, days 3 to 5 | Lesson 2: the fake-message trick and its clues (days 3 and 4); lesson 4: programs that hide inside others (day 3); lesson 6: protection against attacks (day 5) | Lesson 1 You and your data: the value of personal data is grade 7's g7-personal-data. Lesson 3 Script kiddies and lesson 5 There's no place like 127.0.0.1: their titles point to attack tools and attacks run from a machine, and the course runs no attack, and England's computer misuse law is not ours; the store read checks them (For the lead 2) |
| Python programming with sequences of data | 1 Warm up; 5 Make a thing | Unit 2, days 15, 19, 20 and 23 | Lesson 1: the warm-up on lists, as the worked program traced (day 15); lesson 5: the project build (days 19, 20 and 23), our word-guess game in place of its project | Lessons 2 Playlist, 3 In a while, crocodile and 4 The famous for: adapted by the grade 7 frame (its unit 2), so here they are retrieval only. Lesson 6 Wrap up: the unit's own assessment; our check days are ours. Its leaked-password example set is left out (privacy rule) |
| Year 8: Layers of computing systems | 6 (sharing and open source, offered by the grade 7 frame) | Unit 2, day 21 | Sharing code and open licenses, read for the credit and permission cases | Lessons 1 to 5: placed or left out by the grade 7 frame |
