1: # Course frame: Computer Science, Grade 8
2: 
3: Course `computer-science-grade-8` (catalogue id `cs-k12`), grade 8, one year, the last of the three middle school courses. Draft frame, 2026-11-25, after the kindergarten pilot; the scope and sequence sets the days later.
4: 
5: ## The course in brief
6: 
7: - **Units.** 5, each a concept area at its grade 8 depth, in the order the ideas build: systems that hold up
8:   (systems and security, so the year's toy security page, device rules and accessibility tests come first), make it,
9:   credit it, hand it over (programming, the year's text project), data that answers back (data and analysis, after
10:   math grade 8 teaches scatter plots), AI under test (algorithms and machine learning, using unit 3's data and unit 2's
11:   project), and new technology, our town (computing and society, the year's closer).
12: - **Days.** 64 course days: 60 in units, 2 for course-level checks (1 after unit 2, 1 after unit 5) and 2 respond days
13:   (one after each check). Two lessons a week of about 45 minutes, 32 weeks: the computer science default for grades 6
14:   to 12 (docs/courses/computer-science/STANDARD.md section 2, Cory, Desk q-fix-cs-1), an elective, so course days 2k-1
15:   and 2k fall in week k. The 45 minutes hold set-up, clean-up and collecting work: a lesson opens with a five-minute
16:   retrieval trace, a wrong example from the last exit ticket, or a short concrete moment (APPROACH.md, "How a day
17:   opens"), and device lessons keep three minutes at each end to get devices out, saved and put away, so a device
18:   lesson teaches for about 34 minutes and a paper lesson for about 40. Each unit's days include its end-of-unit
19:   assessment and end with a respond day, with one shape for a unit that a course-level check follows directly (the
20:   kindergarten pilot's pattern): in units 2 and 5 the check stands for the unit's own end-of-unit assessment and the
21:   check's respond day is the unit's respond day, so no week holds an assessment and a respond day just before a check.
22:   Units 1 to 5 run 13, 15, 12, 12 and 8 days. Days follow the load of the rows: unit 2 carries the year's multi-week
23:   text project (g8-text-project, "a multi-week text project") with the attribution and refine-for-users rows inside it,
24:   the heaviest load, and gets 15; unit 1 has four systems rows, two of them design-and-test rows, and gets 13; units 3
25:   and 4 own three and four ids with device days and get 12 each; unit 5's four society rows are single unplugged days
26:   in the progression, and with a two-lesson debate get 8. 64 lessons for 18 rows (13 owned ids) is about three and a
27:   half a row, against the kindergarten pilot's two, because grade 8 rows carry a device version, a written product or a
28:   project. No unit runs over 25 days, so there are no mid-unit assessments.
29: - **Check weeks.** Grade 8 checks are kept off core check days (docs/courses/CALENDAR.md, stagger rule 3, grades 6 to 8
30:   by school day; the grade 8 core check school days are civics 48, 49, 96, 97, 145 and 146, ELA 89, 90, 125 and 126,
31:   math 70 and 116, science 75, and week 32's finals days). The mid-year check is course day 29, school day 73, week 15
32:   (after unit 2), with its respond day on course day 30, school day 75 (a respond day, not a check day). The year-end
33:   check is course day 63, school day 158, week 32, the finals week (stagger rule 3), with its respond day on course day
34:   64, school day 160. Unit 5 runs in weeks 28 to 31 before it. Overlaps with other after-core subjects' check days are
35:   the lead's calendar pass across the after-core frames (For the lead 1).
36: - **Standard.** The 13 MS ids the progression gives grade 8 to own, of the 2026 CSTA PK-12 Computer Science Standards:
37:   MS-SYS-SE-33, MS-SYS-IM-36, MS-PRO-PD-14, MS-PRO-TR-20, MS-DAT-DI-25, MS-DAT-DI-27, MS-DAT-IM-29, MS-ALG-ML-08,
38:   MS-ALG-IM-11, MS-ALG-PS-05, MS-PRO-RD-18, MS-SOC-ET-41, MS-SOC-ET-42 (STANDARD.md "Ownership of MS and HS ids", 13 in
39:   grade 8). Grade 8 is the last middle school owner, so every MS id is owned by the end of this year. Five more rows use
40:   seven ids owned in grades 6 and 7 in a new setting (~MS-SYS-NT-35, ~MS-SYS-HW-30, ~MS-ALG-PS-01, ~MS-PRO-RD-17,
41:   ~MS-SOC-HI-39, ~MS-SOC-HU-43, ~MS-SOC-CE-44); they are named in "What students learn" as used again, never in Owned
42:   standards. Each owned id is taught at the depth its owner row's notes set, in our own words (STANDARD.md "Depth"; the
43:   boundary statements are never copied or quoted), and "What students learn" says what is out. Plans cite ids and state
44:   each action in a short phrase of our own (STANDARD.md "Quoting"); the credit line "Standards: 2026 CSTA PK-12
45:   Computer Science Standards (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0)" goes on any day that leans on a statement. Check set: each unit plan
46:   checks itself against the computer science bar (docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md part 5, items 1 to 9) and the lens
47:   (docs/agents/lenses/computer-science.md), and reads the partial base unit it adapts for order; the "Check set adds"
48:   column names what the writer expects that to add. Values rule (DESK-ANSWERS 2026-11-12) holds. No Specialty id, and
49:   no X + CS id, is claimed. Lists go past the middle school boundary (g8-text-project notes, Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk
50:   q-fix-cs-2): the list work in unit 2 is about 4 days (days 15 and 16 and parts of the build days), about 6 percent of
51:   the 64, inside bar criterion C1's 10 percent.
52: - **Practices and concept areas.** The Units table's fifth column (the C3 column of the core frames) names, for
53:   computer science, the concept area each unit teaches and the CSTA practices it teaches inside the content, by code
54:   and our label (STANDARD.md section 3), as bar items 1 and 2 ask. Each unit plan names the practice's day and task.
55:   Across the year: CT6 define the problem (units 1, 3), CT7 abstract and find patterns (units 2, 3), CT8 build (units
56:   2, 4), CT9 test, debug and refine (units 1, 2, 4), IC3 explain and give feedback (units 2, 3, 4, 5), IC4 run a
57:   project (unit 2), IC5 be a reliable teammate (units 1, 2, 5, pair and team roles), HCD10 include different users
58:   (units 1, 5), HCD11 prototype and test for access (units 1, 2), HCD12 keep users informed (unit 4, the model card),
59:   ESR1 weigh who gains and who is harmed (units 3, 4, 5), ESR2 credit others' work and protect users' data (units 1,
60:   2). Dispositions are named in the unit plans, not here.
61: - **Base text.** Partial: England's open computing curriculum, the Teach Computing Curriculum, archived edition
62:   (raspberrypi.org/curriculum/archived, key stage 3, resources dated 2023), which its publisher no longer maintains;
63:   England's current lessons are on Oak National Academy (as the kindergarten frame records). England's Year 9 is the
64:   grade 8 age. License: the scout read the Open Government Licence v3.0 "unless expressly stated otherwise" on the
65:   publisher's terms page; the archive's unit pages carry no license line of their own, so each file's license line is
66:   read when the store folder is made. Adapted unit by unit and rephrased for an American audience (STANDARD.md section
67:   2, Cory 2026-11-16): US spelling, school years named as US grades, no money in pounds and no English law, no product
68:   or company name, our own editor and pages in place of named apps. Three of its six Year 9 units feed units 1, 2 and 3 (two lessons of the Python unit, the rest of which grade 7 adapts), with one Year 8 lesson the grade 7 frame offered;
69:   its animation, audiovisual representation and physical computing units are left out (see Source map). Units 4 and 5
70:   are our own: the key stage 3 curriculum has no AI unit and little of the society strand (docs/reviews/
71:   computer-science-open-material-2026-11-16.md). The source's name appears only in a day's credit line, once a day.
72:   Pending(docs/sources/BASE-TEXTS.md#teach-computing-curriculum): the source store holds no copy of this base, so the
73:   Source map rests on the public unit pages (lesson titles and unit overviews, opened 2026-11-25), and each adapted
74:   lesson's check for third-party images, text and data waits on the store folder (For the lead 2).
75: - **Day kinds.** As docs/GLOSSARY.md (row "teaching day") defines them, in computer science's words (APPROACH.md):
76:   Understanding is an unplugged or trace lesson, the first teaching of a construct or idea (a network map cut and
77:   traced, a worked twenty-line program traced with a trace-table, a dataset followed through its stages on paper).
78:   Fluency is a modify lesson: change a working program, rule or chart in small checked steps (unit 2 days 16 and 17,
79:   unit 4 day 48, and the five-minute retrieval traces). Application is a make, design or society lesson: design and
80:   build a program against criteria, test a system with users' stated needs, run a data investigation, write a
81:   justified verdict or debate with sources (unit 1 days 2, 5, 9 to 11, unit 2 days 18 to 23 and 25 to 27, unit 3 days 34, 35, 37,
82:   38 and 40, unit 4 days 46 and 52, unit 5 days 56 and 58 to 60). Check is the end-of-unit assessment or a course-level
83:   check; Respond acts on it by error letter. Inside a unit: understanding before modify before make, and no make day
84:   before a trace or modify day on the same construct.
85: - **Evidence.** Daily: an exit ticket with a short program or case and "what happens?" or "who does this change?",
86:   answered in writing with wrong answers by error letter; on modify and make days the program itself against two or
87:   three look-fors and one line on what was tested and changed (APPROACH.md, "The form of daily evidence"). Scored: every
88:   end-of-unit assessment is a sitting with unseen items, never the unit's own anchor, of about 35 minutes on paper or
89:   device. Two units also score a product whose draft, written feedback and revision days are counted in the unit:
90:   unit 2's text project (draft day 23, written feedback days 24 and 25, revision days 26 and 27, hand-in day 28, on a
91:   rubric) and unit 3's investigation summary (draft day 37, written feedback and revision day 38). Each owned id has
92:   its own look-for: a "modify" or "refine" id (MS-ALG-IM-11, MS-PRO-TR-20) by a changed rule or program retested, a
93:   "justify", "summarize" or "evaluate" id (MS-PRO-PD-14, MS-DAT-DI-27, MS-ALG-ML-08, MS-SOC-ET-41) by the student's
94:   own written reasoning on a new case, a "debate" id (MS-SOC-ET-42) by a written claim with its backing and a rebuttal,
95:   never by recognition of a supplied answer. Units 2 and 5 are assessed in the course-level check that follows them
96:   (Days). No score comes from a lesson that first teaches a row. The respond day reteaches by error letter and repeats
97:   the missed item in a new form.
98: - **Devices.** One device per pair is the default on a device day, with roles (driver and navigator) that switch at a
99:   stated time and every student typing in every device lesson. Device days: unit 1 days 4 and 8 (a toy inbox and a
100:   practice sign-up page built into our pages), unit 2 days 15 to 17, 19, 20, 22, 23 and 25 to 28 (our editor, Python
101:   text), unit 3 days 32, 33, 34 and 35 (a table-and-scatter tool), unit 4 days 44, 45, 48, 51 and 52 (a classifier test
102:   page, our editor), and the year-end check's code items where devices allow. Every unit keeps at least one unplugged
103:   day (APPROACH.md, Pacing). Every device day is written four ways (STANDARD.md "Devices and rooms"): one device per
104:   pair, a few devices at stations with the other pairs on the paper version, one screen with a student at the device
105:   for each step, or no device. Owned codes that need technology: MS-DAT-DI-25 is met only on a device (STANDARD.md
106:   list); on the no-device path the student plots the points by hand and draws the trend line, and the course record
107:   marks it "part met without a device". MS-ALG-PS-05 is met only on the teacher's one screen with a tool the school has
108:   already approved and no student accounts; a school with none uses Curio Class's saved outputs and marks the id "part
109:   met" (STANDARD.md "Generated-text tools", Cory, Desk q-prework-cs-1). Every other owned code is met in full on paper:
110:   printed test results and a printed model card (MS-ALG-ML-08), printed code samples run by hand on a paper test-table
111:   (MS-PRO-RD-18), a paper prototype of a program's screens tried by two classmates against the stated needs
112:   (MS-PRO-TR-20), a printed sign-up page with its barrier (MS-SYS-IM-36).
113: - **Paper path.** Every lesson has a paper version: programs written and traced on paper and checked by a partner who
114:   is "the computer", trace-tables, list-diagrams, printed tables plotted by hand, printed samples and cards. No tool
115:   asks for a student account, stores a student's name or sends anything off the device (STANDARD.md "Privacy and
116:   safety"); security lessons use toy systems built into the page, with no scanning and no attack on any real system.
117: - **Reading level and vocabulary.** Code read on a page is at most twenty lines (APPROACH.md, Supports). Each unit names
118:   five to eight words taught in depth, with words that have an everyday meaning taught as two meanings (bug, cloud,
119:   model, license, feature). Sentence frames: "I predict ___ because line ___ says ___" and "This decision changes the
120:   experience for ___ because ___". Error messages in our tools are plain words we write. A student may answer a trace
121:   in numbers, arrows or the home language, and each key says which wrong answers can come from reading the words.
122: - **Swap or leave out.** STANDARD.md section 3 lets a school swap or leave out a named unit for its community's
123:   reasons. Unit 5 may be swapped for other emerging technology cases and another local debate topic, or left out:
124:   MS-SOC-ET-41 and MS-SOC-ET-42 are then not claimed that year and the family note names them; the course still meets
125:   computing and society through unit 4's harms and bias rows and unit 2's attribution row. Unit 3 may be swapped for
126:   other supplied data sets, not left out, so the course always claims MS-DAT-DI-25, MS-DAT-DI-27 and MS-DAT-IM-29 (bar
127:   part 5 fails a course with nothing on data). Units 1, 2 and 4 carry the year's security, programming and AI ids and
128:   the project the later units use, and are not swapped or left out.
129: - **Safety.** No lab or field work, so no laboratory or field safety guide applies. Device lessons keep cords behind
130:   the table and devices carried closed with two hands; security lessons run only on toy systems inside our pages,
131:   never on a real school or home system or account. The scope and sequence copies these lines into each device and
132:   security day.
133: - **Costs.** Provisional, for a class of 30, beyond the school's own devices: unit 1 $4 (printing network maps, attack
134:   and prevention cards, the printed sign-up page and user-need cards), unit 2 $5 (printing the worked game program,
135:   trace-tables, test-tables, the credit-case cards and the project rubric), unit 3 $5 (printed tables, graph paper for
136:   the hand plots, the stage cards), unit 4 $4 (printed test results, model cards and code samples), unit 5 $4 (case
137:   cards and two debate source packets), about $22 in all; no consumables and no board.
138: - **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md);
139:   no outside reader is planned (Cory, 2026-11-14). Unit 1 has disability content (users who work by keyboard alone, by
140:   screen reader or with enlarged text) and online safety content (fake messages asking for a password). Before it
141:   reads, the agent studies a starting pack: the W3C Web Accessibility Initiative's "How People with Disabilities Use the
142:   Web" (w3.org/WAI/people-use-web/), the Autistic Self Advocacy Network's "Identity-First Language" page
143:   (autisticadvocacy.org/about-asan/identity-first-language/), one community's own account, not taken as every
144:   community's, and the National Center on Disability and Journalism's Disability Language Style Guide
145:   (ncdj.org/style-guide/), and widens it to the brief's five sources, two of them owned by disabled people's own
146:   organizations, and records them. It checks that no task asks a student to act out a disability (needs are test
147:   conditions, not roles) and that the online safety days carry health's "If a student tells you something" steps word
148:   for word (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md; APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground). Unit 5 touches rural communities, older adults,
149:   families with low incomes and speakers of languages other than English (the video visit case): the agent studies
150:   the federal report itself (the Office of the Assistant Secretary for Planning and Evaluation's 2022 report on
151:   telehealth use in 2021, aspe.hhs.gov), the National Telecommunications and Information Administration's Internet
152:   Use Survey (ntia.gov) and the National Digital Inclusion Alliance's own pages (digitalinclusion.org), and adds a
153:   source from a rural or language community's own organization; it checks that the talk stays on how the service was
154:   designed, never on a classmate's home, health or devices. Unit 5's career day
155:   and unit 4's lottery case can touch families' work and means (APPROACH.md, "Work and automation"): the agent checks
156:   no task asks about a family member's job, money or transport. Unit 3's datasets and unit 4's classifier hold no data
157:   about real or identifiable people. The course's checks get the answer-key agent's read. No unit is hands-on in the
158:   laboratory sense, so no safety agent read is planned beyond the Safety lines above, which the sensitivity read
159:   confirms are on each day.
