se requirements by a partner (~MS-SYS-HW-30, used in a new setting, owned in grade 6). Day 8 (device, practice sign-up page): retrieval of g7-access-differences; the page tried against four users' stated needs as test conditions (keyboard only, text enlarged to twice its size, read without color, plain words for a reader new to English); the barrier found. Day 9 (team design): each team proposes a design change for the barrier on the system-diagram and a paper mock-up. Day 10: teammates test the change against each stated need and record what still fails. Day 11: revise and retest (MS-SYS-IM-36, improve a design together for different users). Out: no real attack, scan or account, no encryption mechanics (grade 9's g9-security-measures compares measures), no protocol detail (grade 9 and 10), no operating system layer (g9-operating-systems). End-of-unit (day 12): a new map with a new failure to redraw with a backup link; three new attacks, each matched to its side and a prevention and the choice explained (SE-33); a new system with a new barrier: a design change for two stated needs and how the team would test it (IM-36); a short justification against new requirements, recorded for the HW-30 thread; respond day 13. Tools: a toy inbox page (messages built into the page, job: practice spotting a fake message with no real account); a practice sign-up page (job: a barrier to find and a change to test, keyboard and zoom testable). Curio Class writes: the network maps, attack and prevention cards, the word page, the requirements sets, the sign-up page and its printed version, the user-need cards | First because it sets the year's rules for shared devices, accounts and toy security pages before the project and the AI days use them, and because its four rows need only grade 7's systems rows, which open as retrieval. The barrier-and-test days teach testing with users' stated needs, which unit 2's refine-for-users days use on the students' own programs. Base: archived Year 9 "Introduction of cybersecurity" lessons 2, 4 and 6 for days 3 to 5 (Source map); the network, system choice and design days are our own | Candidate: kept, the partial base's social engineering lesson order (how the trick works, then the clue to spot it) inside days 3 and 4, zero added days | Disability: needs are test conditions on cards, never a role a student acts out; talk is about what the page lets a person do, never about a classmate; use the wording the sensitivity read settles from its pack. Online safety (days 3 to 5): no real password, account or message is used or asked for; the teacher page carries health's "If a student tells you something" steps word for word, since a fake-message day can bring up a real one a student or family received. The flood attack is described, never run. Devices: cords behind the table, carried closed. Agent reads: sensitivity (the unit 1 pack in Sensitivity reads first). Facts: the fake-message clues are checked against the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency's "Recognize and Report Phishing" page (cisa.gov/secure-our-world/recognize-and-report-phishing, as of 2026-11-25); the district and its network are invented for practice. |

