| Word | What it is | Do not call it | Code name and URL, if different |
|---|---|---|---|
| **course** | One grade of one subject (grade 4 math) or one high school course. What a teacher runs from day 1 to the last | class (worksheets), program, track | The frame slug (`math-grade-4`, `biology`): `docs/courses/<course>/`, `data/plans/<course>/`, `/courses/<course>/`; `data/plans/frames.csv` maps it to its catalogue id, subject and grade. Lag: grade 4 math's plan folder is still `math-4` (`legacy_plan_id`); `data/courses.csv` `course_id` (`math-k8`), the `catalog_id` column, is a subject track across grades, not a course; the worksheet code calls a course-sized list a `unit` |
| **scope and sequence** | Every day of a course's year in order, with the reason for the order, the standards and the pacing (PRODUCTION-PATH step 3, brief docs/pipeline/briefs/sequence-write.md) | spine, year spine, pacing guide (a pacing guide is the calendar only), map | `docs/courses/<course>/scope-and-sequence.md`, day rows in `data/plans/<course>/days.csv`. Lag: older files `docs/courses/<course>-spine.md` |
| **unit** | A run of course days (5 to 42 across the courses; grade 4 math 8 to 22) on one block of standards. A readiness check, given before it opens, precedes it where it assumes an earlier grade row last used two or more grades back; an end-of-unit assessment inside its days checks what it taught, and planned response follows; a unit over 25 days also holds a mid-unit assessment inside its days. The frame and the scope and sequence set its length | stretch, module, topic, chapter | Lag for good: item ids `s11-...` and plan files `sNN.md` keep the `s`; ids are never reused (LESSON-DAY rule 8); `days.csv` `stretch` |
| **section** | A short package of days (1 to 6) inside a unit, with its own plan and opener (section 11.1 is the first section of unit 11) | unit, part (taken by "part of a standard"), topic, lesson set | Lag: folders `courses/<course>/uNN-N/`, `data/units.csv` and its `unit_id` |
| **end-of-unit assessment**, short **unit assessment**; **assessment day** | The assessment near the end of a unit, inside its days, on every part of every standard the unit teaches. It is followed by the response time the plan names, in the same day or on later respond days, counted once. In K to 2 an observation task spread over the unit's last lessons may stand for it | end check, check day, unit test, quiz | `items.csv` `check` = `end`; `days.csv` `kind` = `Check`, `assess` = `end` (scope stage) |
| **mid-unit assessment** | An assessment partway through a unit over 25 days, inside its days (the base texts' own: ELA K to 5, math 6 to 8) | mid-unit check | `days.csv` `assess` = `mid` |
| **course-level check** (mid-year, year-end as adjectives) | An assessment outside every unit that covers several units, set in the frame's Course-level checks table and followed by its respond days | course check, cumulative check, year check, benchmark, year review (review days are their own row) | `days.csv` `stretch` = `C`, `unit` blank, `assess` = `course` |
| **day**, **course day** | One numbered day of a course's year (day 90 of 160), of any kind: teaching, assessment, respond or review. A frame's day totals are course days | lesson day, period, teaching day (that is one kind) | `d090.md`; `/courses/<course>/day-<n>/`; `days.csv` `day` |
| **school day**, **week** | A position in the 160-day, 32-week school year that every course shares (docs/courses/CALENDAR.md): week 1 is school days 1 to 5. A course meeting every day has course day n on school day n; one meeting fewer days spreads its course days over its weeks | date (a school sets its own dates) | `data/plans/calendar.csv`; `days.csv` `school_day` |
| **lesson** | What one teaching day ships: teacher page, student page, exit ticket, answer key, display | lesson day | The template is docs/courses/LESSON-DAY.md (file name lags) |
| **teaching day, assessment day, respond day** | The kinds of day. A teaching day is understanding, fluency or application, in every subject (the goal says the activity: an inquiry, a lab, a discussion, a draft); a respond day acts on an assessment. The kind is what the day does, and this row is the one home of the definitions: **Understanding** is the first explicit teaching of anything new (a skill, concept, method, model, term set, text structure, or a standard's first day in the course), a bridge that teaches ahead of another subject included; **Fluency** is practice of what earlier days taught, so a Fluency day teaches nothing new and drafts, revises and edits no piece of writing; **Application** uses taught work on a new task or case (an inquiry, a lab, a design, a performance), and every day that drafts, revises or edits a piece of writing is Application. Where a day both first-teaches and drafts, first teaching wins (Understanding). Work made on a Fluency day may still be collected and scored later after feedback and revision (unit-lens.md). sequence.py fails the plainest mislabels (settled 2026-11-17, see DESK-ANSWERS) | check day | `days.csv` `kind`: `Understanding`, `Fluency`, `Application`, `Check` (an assessment day), `Respond` |
| **warm-up** | The first minutes of a day (a grammar warm-up in ELA) | opener (that is the section's teacher page), opening, grammar opener | `days.csv` `opens`, `opens_min` |
| **goal** (on screen and paper: the "I can" line) | What the day is for, in student words | objective, target | `days.csv` `goal` |
| **section opener** | The teacher page for a section: the content at an adult's level, why this model, the common errors, the supply list | unit opener, unit overview, intro | `opener.md` in each section folder |
